WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
 By the end of the 1970s, there was quite a sophisticated network. Like I said, they'd

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:10.340
 rewritten it four times. It had gotten to be very sophisticated. There'd been good investment.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:15.600
 They had been careful about understanding how to improve it each time they rebuilt it.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.040
 And so it was a really fine working piece of software. And the people who used it at

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:29.340
 these research universities and at the military, it was kind of like a futuristic world, right?

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.780
 You could send email and a second later it would appear, right? It was really quick and

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.420
 everything was nice. And you could even have instant message-like interactions where it

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:43.460
 went right then. And the problem was, is this was a small group of people. This is maybe

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:49.020
 looks like 60 or so. That was all the computers on the entire Internet, right? I mean, now

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:56.340
 we have our cell phones and we have billions of them, right? But this is like 60. And so

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:03.120
 a real question is, how did this go from 60 computers of a very narrow group to a much

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:13.500
 larger group? And the answer to that question is at Urbana-Champaign, Illinois. And so at

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.820
 the same time that it kind of goes back to Bletchley Park, right? Where the computers

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:25.060
 were part of science. Throughout the 50s and 60s and 70s, scientists, much like the military,

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:29.820
 were realizing computers were mighty useful to advance scientific research. And so the

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.620
 National Science Foundation would fund universities to buy computers. And they'd say, "Give me

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.260
 $10 million to buy a computer." And another university would be like, "Give me $10 million

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.060
 to buy a computer." And then three years later the first university said, "My computer's

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.460
 obsolete. I need another $10 million." Now the research questions were good research

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:51.240
 questions and they were important to society, but the National Science Foundation got tired

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:58.100
 of giving $10 million to everybody. Wait, hang on a second. As a matter of fact, I was

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:06.540
 part of this because I used to be a high-performance computer guy. This is a convex C3800 supercomputer.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.100
 I would be about this tall on this supercomputer. This is a lovely and very expensive model

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.940
 that I was given afterwards. They were going to throw it away because this is from like

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:23.380
 1987. And basically I wanted one of these terribly badly. Each one of these is about

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:29.580
 $2 million. This is like two, four, six, eight, $10 million. And I so badly wanted this and

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:35.700
 I thought that I deserved a $10 million toy and I could do such great research. But unfortunately

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.340
 everybody else wanted the exact same thing. And they were important too. So the National

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.740
 Science Foundation said to themselves, "Hey, this isn't going to work very well if I can't

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:52.860
 find my pen. You know, we're going to make a network. How about we put a few of these

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.300
 things in and then make a network and connect them together?" Of course it's never as simple

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:05.620
 as that. So now we're going to meet Larry Smarr at the National Center for Supercomputing

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:12.820
 Applications, NCSA, at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. And Larry was the director

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:19.180
 of NCSA and one of the many people, but one of the most instrumental people in creating

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.780
 the national network that we now think of as the internet and getting it, moving it

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:32.460
 from being a research project to being a project that we all, both academics and regular people,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:38.300
 we all can can make really good use of it. So let's take a look at Larry Smarr.

